concepts.example:
Illustrative Terraform fragment (requires separately configured provider, variables and networking):
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami = var.approved_ami_id
  instance_type = var.instance_type
  subnet_id = var.private_subnet_id
  vpc_security_group_ids = [aws_security_group.web.id]
}
resource "aws_security_group" "web" {
  name = "web-sg"
  vpc_id = var.vpc_id
  ingress {
    from_port = 443
    to_port = 443
    protocol = "tcp"
    cidr_blocks = [var.trusted_client_cidr]
  }
}
The instance references the security-group ID, creating a dependency edge. The AMI must be valid in the chosen region; a copied sample AMI ID is not portable. This fragment does not allocate a static IP or a full working environment. Review network access and expected replacements in a plan before applying any real infrastructure change.

practice.question:
Given the following Terraform snippet, what actions will "terraform plan" report?

resource "aws_instance" "app" {
  ami           = "ami-12345"
  instance_type = "t2.micro"
  tags = { Name = "app" }
}

# State currently has an instance with the same name but instance_type "t2.small".

practice.answer:
def tf_fmt_success(path):
    import subprocess, os
    try:
        result = subprocess.run(["terraform", "fmt", "-check", path],
                                capture_output=True, text=True, check=True)
        return True
    except subprocess.CalledProcessError:
        return False

The function invokes "terraform fmt -check" which exits with a non‑zero status if any file is not properly formatted. The try/except captures that status and returns a boolean indicating success.
